# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Micah'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'prophets'
SUBTITLE = 'Judgment on Samaria and Jerusalem, and the Mountain of the LORD’s House'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Micah is “the word of the LORD that came to Micah the Morasthite in the days of Jotham, Ahaz, and Hezekiah, kings of Judah, which he saw concerning Samaria and Jerusalem” (Mic. 1:1). It speaks judgment on Samaria and Jerusalem, promises that “in the last days” the mountain of the house of the LORD shall be established, names the ruler to come from Bethlehem, and closes with a prayer to the God who pardons iniquity (Mic. 3:12; 4:1; 5:2; 7:18).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD speaks to Jacob and Israel, to Zion and Jerusalem, about judgment, the remnant, the ruler from Bethlehem and the last days (Mic. 3:8; 4:1–2; 5:3; 7:20).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Micah', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Judgment on Samaria and Jerusalem (Chs. 1–3)'), ('p', 'The LORD comes against Samaria and Jerusalem, and Zion is plowed as a field (Mic. 1:1; 3:8, 12).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Last Days (Ch. 4)'), ('p', 'The mountain of the LORD’s house is established, the nations go up, and every man sits under his vine and fig tree (Mic. 4:1–4).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The Ruler From Bethlehem (Ch. 5)'), ('p', 'A ruler comes forth out of Bethlehem Ephratah, and the remnant of his brethren return (Mic. 5:3–5).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — The LORD’s Controversy (Ch. 6)'), ('p', 'The LORD pleads with His people and shows what He requires (Mic. 6:8).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — Waiting and Pardon (Ch. 7)'), ('p', 'The prophet waits for the God of his salvation and closes with the God who pardons (Mic. 7:7, 9, 18–20).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Micah', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>Judgment Declared</b> — The LORD’s word is “concerning Samaria and Jerusalem” (Mic. 1:1).', '<b>Zion Plowed</b> — “Zion for your sake be plowed as a field, and Jerusalem shall become heaps” (Mic. 3:12).', '<b>The Last Days</b> — “In the last days it shall come to pass, that the mountain of the house of the LORD shall be established” (Mic. 4:1).', '<b>The Law From Zion</b> — “The law shall go forth of Zion, and the word of the LORD from Jerusalem” (Mic. 4:2).', '<b>War No More</b> — “Nation shall not lift up a sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more” (Mic. 4:3).', '<b>The Remnant Return</b> — “The remnant of his brethren shall return unto the children of Israel” (Mic. 5:3).', '<b>The Oath to the Fathers</b> — “Thou wilt perform the truth to Jacob, and the mercy to Abraham” (Mic. 7:20).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Micah', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Micah points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Ruler From Bethlehem</b> — The ruler whose goings forth are from of old comes out of Bethlehem Ephratah (Mic. 5:2); the chief priests and scribes quote the place in Matthew 2:5–6.', '<b>He Shall Be the Peace</b> — “This man shall be the peace” (Mic. 5:5), and “he shall stand and feed in the strength of the LORD” (Mic. 5:4).', '<b>A Kingdom Without War</b> — The nations’ swords are beaten into plowshares in the last days (Mic. 4:3), reported within Israel’s kingdom hope.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Micah', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Micah speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Micah operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Micah in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Micah reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['Jacob and Israel told their transgression (Mic. 3:8)', 'Zion and Jerusalem, and the mountain of the LORD’s house (Mic. 3:12; 4:1–2)', 'the remnant of his brethren returning to the children of Israel (Mic. 5:3)', 'the truth to Jacob and the mercy to Abraham sworn to the fathers (Mic. 7:20)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Micah that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Micah contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Word Concerning Samaria — “which he saw concerning Samaria and Jerusalem.” (Mic. 1:1).', 'Jacob Told His Sin — “to declare unto Jacob his transgression, and to Israel his sin.” (Mic. 3:8).', 'Zion Plowed — “Zion for your sake be plowed as a field.” (Mic. 3:12).', 'The Mountain Established — “the mountain of the house of the LORD shall be established in the top of the mountains.” (Mic. 4:1).', 'The Law From Zion — “the law shall go forth of Zion, and the word of the LORD from Jerusalem.” (Mic. 4:2).', 'War No More — “nation shall not lift up a sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more.” (Mic. 4:3).', 'Vine and Fig Tree — “they shall sit every man under his vine and under his fig tree.” (Mic. 4:4).', 'The Remnant Return — “the remnant of his brethren shall return unto the children of Israel.” (Mic. 5:3).', 'He Shall Feed — “he shall stand and feed in the strength of the LORD.” (Mic. 5:4).', 'The Truth to Jacob — “Thou wilt perform the truth to Jacob, and the mercy to Abraham.” (Mic. 7:20).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Micah Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Micah and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Micah teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Micah', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Word Concerning Samaria', '“which he saw concerning Samaria and Jerusalem.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mic. 1:1'], ['Jacob Told His Sin', '“to declare unto Jacob his transgression, and to Israel his sin.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mic. 3:8'], ['Zion Plowed', '“Zion for your sake be plowed as a field.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mic. 3:12'], ['The Mountain Established', '“the mountain of the house of the LORD shall be established in the top of the mountains.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mic. 4:1'], ['The Law From Zion', '“the law shall go forth of Zion, and the word of the LORD from Jerusalem.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mic. 4:2'], ['War No More', '“nation shall not lift up a sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mic. 4:3'], ['Vine and Fig Tree', '“they shall sit every man under his vine and under his fig tree.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mic. 4:4'], ['The Remnant Return', '“the remnant of his brethren shall return unto the children of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mic. 5:3'], ['He Shall Feed', '“he shall stand and feed in the strength of the LORD.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mic. 5:4'], ['The Truth to Jacob', '“Thou wilt perform the truth to Jacob, and the mercy to Abraham.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Mic. 7:20']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Micah that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Micah teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['What the LORD Requires', '“to do justly, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with thy God.”', 'Mic. 6:8'], ['Waiting on God', '“I will wait for the God of my salvation: my God will hear me.”', 'Mic. 7:7'], ['Bearing Discipline', '“I will bear the indignation of the LORD, because I have sinned against him.”', 'Mic. 7:9'], ['A God Like No Other', '“Who is a God like unto thee, that pardoneth iniquity.”', 'Mic. 7:18'], ['Sins Cast Away', '“thou wilt cast all their sins into the depths of the sea.”', 'Mic. 7:19']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Micah without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Micah in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Do justly</b> — Love mercy and walk humbly (Mic. 6:8).', '<b>Wait on God</b> — The God of my salvation will hear (Mic. 7:7).', '<b>Bear His discipline humbly</b> — Until He pleads my cause (Mic. 7:9).', '<b>Marvel at His pardon</b> — Who is a God like unto thee (Mic. 7:18).', '<b>Rest in His forgiveness</b> — Sins cast into the depths of the sea (Mic. 7:19).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Micah', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Micah in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Judgment on Samaria and Jerusalem (Chs. 1–3) — Zion plowed. <i>(Mic. 1:1; 3:8, 12)</i>', 'The Last Days (Ch. 4) — The mountain of the LORD’s house. <i>(Mic. 4:1–4)</i>', 'The Ruler From Bethlehem (Ch. 5) — The remnant return. <i>(Mic. 5:3–5)</i>', 'The LORD’s Controversy (Ch. 6) — What He requires. <i>(Mic. 6:8)</i>', 'Waiting and Pardon (Ch. 7) — A God who pardons. <i>(Mic. 7:7, 9, 18–20)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Micah, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward justice, mercy, humility, and hope in the LORD without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Micah simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Micah stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
